Here's a simple mermaid diagram illustrating the flow of the AMA project when answering user questions:

```mermaid
graph TD;
    A[User] -->|Asks Question| B[AMA Interface]
    B --> C{Profiles}
    C -->|Analyst| D[Analyze Question]
    C -->|Developer| D
    C -->|Java Specialist| D
    D --> E[Project Files Management]
    E --> F[Knowledge Base]
    F -->|Search and Index| G[Retrieve Information]
    G --> H[Return Answers and References]
    H --> A

    style A fill:#f9f,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    style B fill:#bbf,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    style C fill:#cff,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    style D fill:#fcf,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    style E fill:#ffc,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    style F fill:#cfc,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    style G fill:#ffd,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    style H fill:#ccf,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
```

### Explanation:

1. **User**: The flow starts when a user asks a question.
2. **AMA Interface**: The question is received by the AMA interface.
3. **Profiles**: Determines which profile to apply (Analyst, Developer, Java Specialist) based on the context of the question.
4. **Analyze Question**: Uses the chosen profile to analyze and understand the question.
5. **Project Files Management**: Manages and organizes the relevant project files.
6. **Knowledge Base**: The question is processed using a vector database that performs a full-text search.
7. **Retrieve Information**: Retrieves relevant information and references from the knowledge base.
8. **Return Answers and References**: Finally, answers and references are delivered back to the user, completing the flow.

This diagram visually represents how the AMA project processes user questions to provide accurate and comprehensive answers.
